\paragraph{Re-encoding as general mechanism.}
Both cross-attention and parameter-matched MLP modules achieve comparable recovery ($p = 0.15$), suggesting that learned re-encoding in general overcomes pixel-shuffle entanglement. The relatively stable M-curve ($M{=}16$: 56.9\% vs.\ $M{=}128$: 58.0\%) with a modest dip at $M{=}96$ (54.5\%, $p{=}0.034$ vs.\ $M{=}64$; statistically significant but within practical tolerance) confirms that token count is secondary once entanglement is broken. The contribution is diagnostic: understanding \emph{why} re-encoding works for pixel-shuffle connectors, rather than proposing a general-purpose compression module (Appendix~\ref{app:reencoding_analysis}).

\paragraph{Empirical novelty of pixel-shuffle sensitivity.}
While the \emph{direction} of pixel-shuffle vulnerability may seem intuitive, neither its \emph{magnitude} (5\% removal $\to$ 0\% SR across seven tested configurations) nor its consistency across scoring methods, retention ratios, and mechanisms (learned vs.\ random, zero-mask vs.\ true removal vs.\ merging) was established prior to this work. No previous study has empirically tested pixel-shuffle pruning in closed-loop VLA control.

